Linearizability enforces a global, real-time total order of operations. Every operation appears to execute atomically at a single point in time between its invocation and its completion. In contrast, Sequential Consistency relaxes real-time constraints; operations must be ordered consistently across all observers, but that order does not need to match physical wall-clock time.


Consensus Algorithms
State Machine Replication (SMR) requires distributed nodes to agree on a sequence of inputs and state transitions. Consensus algorithms ensure that a group of machines can survive the failures of some of its members without breaking safety properties.
