Leader Election:
Nodes begin as Followers. If a follower receives no communications within an electionTimeout (randomized between 150ms–300ms to prevent split votes), it transitions to Candidate state.
The Candidate increments its currentTerm, votes for itself, and broadcasts RequestVote RPCs.
A node grants its vote if the candidate's term is greater than or equal to its own, and the candidate's log is at least as up-to-date as its own (lastLogTerm and lastLogIndex check).
Once a candidate receives votes from a strict quorum ($(N/2) + 1$), it transitions to Leader and initiates periodic AppendEntries heartbeats.
Log Replication & Commit Mechanics:
Client sends commands to the Leader.
Leader appends the command to its local log as an uncommitted entry.
Leader issues AppendEntries RPCs containing the entry, alongside prevLogIndex and prevLogTerm.
Followers verify that their logs match at prevLogIndex and prevLogTerm. If verified, they append the entry and return success. If an inconsistency is detected, the Follower rejects the request, causing the Leader to decrement nextIndex and retry until the logs align.
Once the Leader receives replication confirmations from a quorum, the entry is marked as Committed. The Leader applies the log to its local state machine and returns the result to the client.
Subsequent AppendEntries RPCs piggyback the updated leaderCommit index, informing Followers to apply committed entries to their local state machines.
Paxos & Multi-Paxos Execution
Classic Paxos resolves agreement on a single value through a two-phase protocol:[ Proposer ]                       [ Acceptor Quorum ]

     |                                      |

     | ---- Phase 1a: Prepare(n) ---------> |

     | <--- Phase 1b: Promise(n, v_max) --- |  (Quorum reached)

     |                                      |

     | ---- Phase 2a: Accept(n, v) -------> |

     | <--- Phase 2b: Accepted ------------ |  (Quorum reached -> Value Committed)

Phase 1a (Prepare): Proposer chooses a unique proposal number $n$ and broadcasts Prepare(n) to a majority of Acceptors.
Phase 1b (Promise): Acceptors compare $n$ to the highest proposal number they have seen. If $n$ is higher, they return a promise not to accept any future proposals less than $n$, returning the highest-numbered proposal and value they have previously accepted ($n_v, v_a$).
Phase 2a (Accept): If the Proposer receives promises from a quorum, it issues an Accept(n, v) message. $v$ is chosen as the value corresponding to the highest $n_v$ reported in the responses, or any arbitrary value if no acceptor has previously accepted a value.
Phase 2b (Accepted): Acceptors accept Accept(n, v) unless they have processed a Prepare(n') where $n' > n$.

Multi-Paxos Optimization: Classic Paxos requires two full network round-trips for every committed value. Multi-Paxos optimizes this by electing a stable leader via Phase 1 once for a stream of log instances. Subsequent operations require only Phase 2 (Accept $\rightarrow$ Accepted), reducing steady-state round-trip latency by 50%.


Clock Synchronization & Temporal Ordering
Physical clocks in distributed systems are fundamentally unreliable due to thermal variations, crystal oscillator defects, and Network Time Protocol (NTP) synchronization skew, which commonly introduces dynamic drift ranging from a few milliseconds to several seconds.
Physical vs Logical Clocks
Lamport Timestamps: Assign a scalar clock value $C(e)$ to every event.
Local events increment $C_i = C_i + 1$.
Messages include $C_msg = C_i$.
On message receipt, $C_j = \max(C_j, C_msg) + 1$.
Limitation: Lamport timestamps establish a partial order ($a \rightarrow b \implies C(a) < C(b)$), but $C(a) < C(b)$ does not imply $a \rightarrow b$ (cannot detect concurrency).
Vector Clocks: Every process maintains a vector $V$ of size $N$ (where $N$ is the total count of processes).
Local process $i$ increments its entry: $V_i[i] = V_i[i] + 1$.
Messages carry the entire vector $V_i$.
On receipt at process $j$: $V_j[k] = \max(V_j[k], V_msg[k])$ for all $k$, and $V_j[j] = V_j[j] + 1$.
Vector clocks accurately track causality and identify concurrent operations ($V(a) \not< V(b) \land V(b) \not< V(a)$).
