                     Uncertainty Bound: +/- epsilon

TrueTime exposes an explicit API method TT.now(), returning a time interval $[t_{earliest}, t_{latest}]$ where $t_{latest} - t_{earliest} = 2\epsilon$. The system dynamically maintains $\epsilon$ (typically under 1ms, expanding to tens of milliseconds if time sync hardware fails).

To maintain absolute linearizability across cross-region distributed transactions, Spanner uses Commit Wait:
Before committing a transaction $T_1$ with assigned commit timestamp $t_{commit} = \text{TT.now}().latest$, the leader node blocks execution until $\text{TT.now}().earliest > t_{commit}$. This guarantees that no subsequent transaction $T_2$ anywhere in the global topology can receive a commit timestamp $t_2 \le t_1$, ensuring linearizable order without inter-node clock synchronization round-trips during transaction evaluation.


Eventual Consistency & Conflict Resolution
In eventual consistency models, replicas may temporarily diverge under concurrent writes but are guaranteed to converge to identical state once all updates have propagated.
Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)
CRDTs enable automatic state convergence without central coordination. They are mathematically structured to guarantee that state operations commute, associate, and are idempotent.

Operation-Based CRDTs (CmRDT): Replicate operations over the wire. Requires an underlying network layer that guarantees causal, exactly-once delivery of operation payload streams.
State-Based CRDTs (CvRDT): Replicate full local states. Replicas merge incoming states using a bounded semi-lattice join operator ($\sqcup$). Convergence relies on the join operator meeting three structural properties:
Commutativity: $A \sqcup B = B \sqcup A$
Associativity: $(A \sqcup B) \sqcup C = A \sqcup (B \sqcup C)$
Idempotency: $A \sqcup A = A$
Alternative Reconciliation Strategies
Last-Write-Wins (LWW): Resolves conflicts by comparing wall-clock timestamps attached to concurrent updates. It drops older writes completely. LWW is susceptible to clock drift, which can lead to silent data loss (an earlier logical write with a higher physical clock timestamp will permanently overwrite a later logical write).
Vector Clock Reconciliations: Stores concurrent branch histories when a concurrent write occurs. The system defers resolution to read time, delegating application-specific reconciliation algorithms or client-side conflict selection (e.g., Riak siblings, Amazon Dynamo shopping carts).


