Detailed Execution Sequence
Client Request Routing: A client issues a write statement in the EU region (UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A102'). The request reaches the local EU-Central gateway node.
Range Index Lookups: The EU gateway queries its local Range Metadata cache. It determines that key 'A102' falls within Range 402, whose current Raft Leader and Leaseholder reside on Node 101 in US-East.
Network Transit to Leaseholder: The EU node routes the SQL command payload over a persistent gRPC tunnel to Node 101 in US-East.
Transaction Planning & Concurrency Control: Node 101 checks its local concurrency control structures (e.g., Read/Write Intents stored alongside keys in an LSM-tree). It acquires a latch on key 'A102' to serialize concurrent local operations.
Raft Proposal Generation: Node 101 acts as the Raft Leader for Range 402. It construct a new Raft log entry representing the update, increments the Raft Index, assigns a transactional read/write commit evaluation timestamp based on its local HLC (Hybrid Logical Clock), and appends the uncommitted entry to its local Raft log.
Parallel Replication: Node 101 issues an AppendEntries RPC in parallel to all Followers in the consensus group: Node 202 (US-West) and Node 303 (EU-Central).
Quorum Acknowledgement:
Node 101 writes to its disk locally.
Node 202 (US-West) receives the payload, writes the entry to its local Write-Ahead Log (WAL), and responds with a success acknowledgement over cross-continental network transit.
Node 303 (EU-Central) receives the entry later due to higher network propagation delays.
Once Node 101 receives confirmation from Node 202, it satisfies the strict consensus quorum requirement ($2/3$ nodes confirmed).
Commit Phase & Intent Resolution: Node 101 marks the log entry as Committed. It applies the state change to its underlying storage engine (converting the write intent into an inline value in the underlying Pebble/RocksDB LSM-tree) and broadcasts an updated leaderCommit pointer to Node 202 and Node 303 in the background.
Response Propagation: Node 101 returns execution success back down the network link to the initiating gateway node in EU-Central, which then transmits the HTTP/gRPC success response to the client application.


