Bounded Context: A explicit boundary within which a domain model applies. Language constructs (e.g., "Account", "Order", "Item") have precise, unambiguous meanings only inside their specific context.
Entities: Domain objects defined by a persistent unique identity (e.g., CustomerID, OrderID), maintaining continuity throughout their lifecycle regardless of changes to their structural attributes.
Value Objects: Immutable objects characterized purely by their attributes (e.g., Address, Money). They possess no conceptual identity and are fully interchangeable.
Aggregate Root: A cluster of associated domain objects (Entities and Value Objects) bound together by a root entity interface. External objects can only hold references to the aggregate root, enforcing all domain invariants for the entire cluster.


