Cache Stampede Mitigation (Probabilistic Early Expiration)
A Cache Stampede occurs when a hot cache key expires, causing hundreds of concurrent client requests to hit the primary underlying database simultaneously.

To prevent this, the system uses XFetch (Probabilistic Early Expiration). Instead of waiting for an absolute TTL expiration, read requests probabilistically calculate whether to recompute and refresh the cache entry before it expires:

$$\text{Read Exception Test: } -\beta \times \delta \times \ln(\text{random}()) > \text{TTL} - \text{now}$$

$\delta$: Time required to compute and update the cache value.
$\beta$: Constant multiplier ($> 0$, aggressive compute factor).
$\text{random}()$: Uniform random value generated in the range $(0, 1]$.

As the remaining TTL approaches zero, the probability of a client asynchronously computing and updating the cached value approaches $100%$, smoothing out database read operations and preventing high load spikes.


