TLS Fingerprinting (JA3/JA4): Inspects low-level details of incoming client ClientHello packets (e.g., cipher suites, TLS version, extensions, elliptic curves). It creates a hash fingerprint (JA3) to identify automated scrapers and headless browsers, even if they spoof user-agent HTTP headers.
Behavioral Anomaly Analysis: Tracks client access patterns over time. Sudden spikes in requests to login endpoints, high ratios of 404 errors, or unnatural page navigation pathways trigger automated challenge mechanisms.
Rate Limiting Mechanics:
Token Bucket Algorithm: Tokens are added to a bucket at a constant fill rate $r$, up to a maximum capacity $b$. Incoming requests consume a token. If the bucket is empty, the request is rejected with HTTP status 429 Too Many Requests. This algorithm naturally handles short, controlled bursts of traffic.
Sliding Window Log Algorithm: Stores timestamps for every incoming request in a Redis sorted set. When a new request arrives, it removes entries older than the defined window ($t - \text{window_size}$) and counts the remaining items. This eliminates boundary edge vulnerability spikes seen in fixed window algorithms.


