Bulkhead Pattern: Isolates critical application resources into isolated pools (e.g., separate thread pools, dedicated memory limits, distinct connection pools for different callers). A failure or resource exhaustion event in a low-priority processing pool cannot consume resources allocated to high-priority execution paths.
Load Shedding: When a service CPU usage, latency targets, or queue depths exceed safety thresholds, it intentionally sheds traffic by shedding low-priority requests (returning 503 Service Unavailable or 429 Too Many Requests) to keep the core system operational.
Backpressure Propagation: Allows downstream components to signal upstream callers to reduce their transmission rate when buffers begin to fill. In gRPC streams, flow control frames restrict upstream sending windows, propagating backpressure through the system call chain all the way back to the ingress edge.


