Service Level Indicator (SLI): A quantifiable metric measuring real-time system performance (e.g., ratio of successful requests to total requests, evaluated over a rolling 30-day window).
Service Level Objective (SLO): The target availability goal for an SLI (e.g., $99.9%$ of requests must complete successfully with a latency under $200\text{ms}$).
Service Level Agreement (SLA): A legal contract defining financial remedies or penalties if the system fails to meet specified SLO thresholds.
Error Budget Mechanics: The allowable margin for service degradation ($100% - \text{SLO}$). If an application has a $99.9%$ availability SLO, its budget allows for $0.1%$ errors. If deployment updates consume the entire error budget within a given window, automated deployment pipelines block future releases, forcing engineering resources to focus exclusively on stability and reliability improvements.


