Step-by-Step Architectural Pipeline Execution
Edge Ingress & Threat Protection:
Global client applications issue requests to an Anycast network ingress endpoint.
The ingress layer performs JA4 TLS fingerprinting to identify and block unauthorized scraper bots.
Requests are throttled using a Redis-backed sliding window rate-limiter, and valid traffic is passed through via HTTP/3 over QUIC.
Zero Trust Authentication & Gateway Mesh:
Traffic reaches the API Gateway, which authenticates incoming user JWTs.
The gateway injects a W3C traceparent context header into every request to ensure end-to-end distributed trace tracking via OpenTelemetry.
Requests enter the internal service mesh using encrypted mTLS tunnels authenticated with short-lived SPIFFE/SPIRE certificates.
High-Throughput Order Ingestion:
Transactions are forwarded over gRPC to the core Transaction Processing Service.
The service checks local in-memory caches to validate user limits and balances without causing database read amplification.
Distributed Storage Consensus Execution:
Validated transactions are executed against a multi-region distributed SQL database (CockroachDB).
The localized Leaseholder node constructs a new Raft log entry and replicates it across regions using Raft consensus.
Once a quorum of replicas confirms the log entry, the data is committed to disk in an LSM storage engine (Pebble/RocksDB).
Change Data Capture & Event Pipeline Processing:
The database's underlying Write-Ahead Log (WAL) streams commit records directly to a Debezium CDC pipeline.
Debezium converts database mutations into structured domain events, streaming them directly into an Apache Kafka cluster.
Kafka partitions events by account_id to guarantee strictly ordered event processing per customer.
Parallel Event Consumption & Data Fanout:
Analytics Consumer Mesh: Ingests raw events from Kafka and writes them in batches directly into a ClickHouse columnar storage cluster for real-time risk reporting and HTAP analytics.
Notification Fanout Mesh: Reads events from Kafka and broadcasts real-time execution updates directly to the WebTransport Edge Worker Cluster.
Real-Time Edge Streaming Delivery:
Edge WebTransport servers receive the outbound notifications and push updates directly to connected client devices with sub-10ms latency.
If downstream network congestion occurs, QUIC flow control backpressure signals automatically slow down event processing streams, protecting edge servers from memory exhaustion while maintaining system stability under high load.

